    "content": "\n         <p>QR menus have transitioned from a pandemic necessity to a permanent fixture in modern Indian hospitality. Not only do they save thousands of rupees in printing costs every time you update your prices, but they also empower guests to order at their own pace, naturally increasing average ticket sizes by up to 15%.</p>\n         <h2>Step 1: Digitize Your Menu Architecture</h2>\n         <p>The first step is not just uploading a PDF. A PDF is clunky and requires zooming. You need a digital, interactive menu. Categorize your items logically (e.g., Starters, Main Course, Breads, Beverages). High-quality imagery is crucial here—dishes with professional photos sell 30% more than text-only listings.</p>\n         <h2>Step 2: Generate and Place the QR Codes</h2>\n         <p>Once your digital menu is live, generate unique QR codes for each table. This allows the POS system to automatically map incoming orders to the correct table number. Print these on durable materials—acrylic standees or laminated vinyl stickers placed at the corner of the table work best. Avoid placing them dead center where spills are most common.</p>\n         <h2>Step 3: Staff Training</h2>\n         <p>Your staff must become advocates for the QR system. Train them to say, \"Welcome! You can scan this code to view photos of our dishes and place your order directly. I'll be right here if you have any questions.\" This bridges the gap between digital efficiency and warm hospitality.</p>\n         <h2>Step 4: Managing the Transition</h2>\n         <p>During the first week, keep a few physical menus on hand for older guests or those whose phones are out of battery. However, make the digital experience so frictionless (no app downloads, fast loading speeds) that 90% of your guests prefer it.</p>\n       "
