    "title": "GST Billing for Restaurants in India — What Every Owner Must Know",
    "summary": "GST compliance trips up many restaurant owners in India. We break down CGST vs SGST vs IGST, which rate applies to dine-in vs takeaway, how to structure your POS invoices correctly, and what a GST audit looks for.",
    "category": "Finance",
    "readTime": "7 min read",
    "date": "May 06, 2026",
    "image": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1552566626-52f8b828add9?auto=format&fit=crop&q=80&w=800",
    "slug": "gst-billing-for-restaurants-in-india-what-every-owner-must-know",
    "content": "<p>GST compliance trips up restaurant owners more than almost any other tax in India. The rules are clear on paper, but the day-to-day reality of dine-in versus takeaway versus aggregator orders gets messy fast. This guide breaks down exactly what you need to know to issue compliant invoices and survive an audit.</p><h2>CGST, SGST, IGST — the three letters every owner gets confused about</h2><p>When you bill a customer who's sitting in your restaurant in the same state where you're registered, the tax splits 50/50 between Central GST (CGST) and State GST (SGST). On a ₹500 food bill at 5%, that's ₹12.50 CGST + ₹12.50 SGST. If the customer is from another state — relevant for online deliveries you ship interstate — it becomes a single Integrated GST (IGST) line for the full ₹25.</p><p>For 95% of restaurants, you'll only ever see CGST + SGST on your invoices. IGST only kicks in for genuine interstate supply, which restaurants rarely do directly.</p><h2>Which rate applies — 5% or 18%?</h2><p>This is where most owners get tripped up. The Indian GST council uses two rates for restaurant services:</p><ul><li><strong>5% (without input tax credit)</strong>: applies to standalone restaurants, both AC and non-AC, including dine-in, takeaway, and home delivery. This is the default for the vast majority of independent restaurants and cafes.</li><li><strong>18% (with input tax credit)</strong>: applies only to restaurants located inside hotels where the room tariff exceeds ₹7,500 per night. If you're not inside a luxury hotel, you almost certainly fall under 5%.</li></ul><p>Alcoholic beverages are taxed separately at state-level VAT (not GST) — your POS needs to handle these as a distinct line item with no GST applied.</p><h2>What every GST invoice must contain</h2><p>A compliant tax invoice (Section 31 of the CGST Act) needs these fields. Your POS should auto-fill them on every bill:</p><ul><li>Your restaurant's name, address, and GSTIN</li><li>Invoice number — sequential, unique per financial year</li><li>Invoice date and time</li><li>Customer name (only mandatory if bill exceeds ₹50,000, but always good practice)</li><li>HSN/SAC code — restaurant services use SAC 996331</li><li>Description of each item, quantity, unit price</li><li>Taxable value, then CGST + SGST shown separately with rate %</li><li>Final invoice total in figures and words</li></ul><p>If any of these are missing, the input tax credit chain breaks for B2B customers and your invoice is not legally compliant for an audit.</p><h2>Dine-in vs takeaway — same rate, different treatment</h2><p>Many owners ask if takeaway should be billed differently. The answer: same 5% rate, but the tax invoice must clearly state \"Takeaway\" or \"Parcel\" in the description so the GST officer can map each transaction to the right service type during reconciliation.</p><p>Some restaurants try to split the bill artificially — charging \"food\" at one rate and \"service\" at another. This is non-compliant. A composite restaurant service is a single supply taxed at the applicable rate.</p><h2>Aggregator orders (Swiggy, Zomato) — who collects the GST?</h2><p>Under Section 9(5) of the CGST Act (effective 1 January 2022), the aggregator collects and deposits GST on restaurant service supplied through them. You as the restaurant do NOT charge GST on the aggregator commission portion — Swiggy/Zomato handle that directly with the customer.</p><p>However, you still need to report these as exempt supplies in your GSTR-1 filing. Your POS must distinguish aggregator-channel orders from direct dine-in/QR menu orders for this report to be accurate.</p><h2>What a GST audit actually looks at</h2><p>If you're selected for audit (random or based on filing irregularities), the GST officer will typically ask for:</p><ol><li>Invoice numbering — gaps, duplicates, or non-sequential numbers raise immediate flags</li><li>Reconciliation between GSTR-1 (sales) and GSTR-3B (summary) for every month</li><li>Sample invoices for high-value days to verify rate and amount accuracy</li><li>Aggregator settlement statements vs your declared exempt supplies</li><li>Cash sales vs declared turnover (officers compare your declared turnover with your electricity bills, rent, staff count — a 200-cover restaurant declaring ₹2 lakh monthly revenue is going to face questions)</li></ol><h2>How modern POS software keeps you audit-ready</h2><p>Issuing a compliant invoice manually for every bill is impossible at scale. Your POS should:</p><ul><li>Generate sequential invoice numbers automatically — no gaps possible</li><li>Apply the right tax rate based on dine-in / takeaway / aggregator channel</li><li>Print HSN/SAC and GSTIN on every bill without manual entry</li><li>Export GSTR-1 ready CSV/JSON files monthly</li><li>Maintain a complete daily Z-report (the closing summary an officer asks for first)</li></ul><p>This is exactly why moving from a paper KOT book or a basic billing machine to a proper cloud POS pays for itself the first time you face an audit query.</p><h2>Bottom line</h2><p>GST for restaurants is more straightforward than people think — 5% for almost everyone, CGST + SGST split, with proper invoice fields. The complexity is in execution: hundreds of bills per day, multiple channels, and the discipline to keep every record clean. That's a software problem, not a tax problem. Get the software right and your GST life becomes a 10-minute monthly task instead of a quarterly panic.</p>"
